# Copy to lb/.env (gitignored, chmod 600) and fill in. Compose reads it for interpolation and hands
# every line to the vllm-lb container. A commented line shows what the code does when it is absent.
# The example addresses are RFC 5737 TEST-NET placeholders, not a fleet.

# ── required: compose refuses to start without it ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# The secret SearXNG (the web-search sidecar) signs with. Generate one: openssl rand -hex 32
SEARXNG_SECRET=
# THE NAMES THE GATEWAY ANSWERS TO (vllm_lb/host_guard.py) — the DNS-rebinding guard. A page you open elsewhere can
# rebind its own hostname to 127.0.0.1 and then reach this port same-origin; what it cannot forge is its name, which
# the browser sends as Host. So a request's Host must be one of the listener's loopback names (127.0.0.1, localhost,
# [::1] at LB_LISTEN's port, 8010) or a name below, or it is answered 421; a write whose Origin is neither is 403.
# REQUIRED, no default (compose and the LB both refuse without it): `loopback` when nothing but this box reaches the
# LB on its own port; otherwise comma-separated host[:port] — a name with no port matches :80/:443 and no port:
#   vllm.example.com           the Host your reverse proxy / tunnel presents (nginx `proxy_set_header Host $host`
#                              sends the name with no port; an frp http proxy passes it unchanged)
#   vllm-lb:8010               containers that dial the LB by its docker name: a chat front-end, a client on
#                              docker-compose.clients.yml's network
#   lb:8010                    the sealed swarm runner (lb/bench_runner): it calls http://lb:8010 through its relay
#   host.docker.internal:8010  the legacy dashboard (its LB_URL)
#   127.0.0.1:18010            a host port other than 8010: a remapped `ports:` line, or an `ssh -L` to another local port
# The gateway logs each distinct Host once, at first sight ("[host] first request with Host ..."): a refused one
# names this variable, so `docker logs vllm-lb | grep '\[host\]'` lists exactly what reaches it.
LB_ALLOWED_HOSTS=

# ── broker_parrot: OPT-IN ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OFF (the default — the line absent, or anything but 1/true/yes): the LB never imports
# broker_parrot, never touches its DB, asks for no lease or reservation. Every live backend
# configured below is routable as it stands, a turn never waits for a claim, and a cluster
# Start launches on its memory and availability gates alone. `docker compose up` needs no
# broker checkout, no Postgres and no other project's network.
# ON: leased-only routing through broker_parrot (README.md). Set BOTH lines, and the PG_* below:
# LB_BROKER_ENABLED=1
# COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:docker-compose.broker.yml
# docker-compose.broker.yml mounts the engine read-only at /opt/broker_parrot, wires the DB and
# joins the broker's network. Every value below is REQUIRED with the override (compose refuses the
# start naming the missing one) and read only with it: no deployment's checkout, network or database
# name is compiled in.
# LB_BROKER_CHECKOUT=/home/user/broker_parrot   # the broker_parrot checkout the fleet runs (mounted read-only)
# LB_BROKER_NETWORK=broker_default              # the broker project's Docker network (external), where its Postgres is `postgres`
# PG_USER=                                      # the broker's Postgres — the same values as the broker project's own .env
# PG_PW=
# PG_DB=
# LB_DB_URL_ENV=LB_BROKER_DB_URL        # the name of the variable that holds the broker's DB URL

# ── the sealed swarm runner's network: ONLY with the broker override ──────────────────────────
# With the broker on, the LB's only network is the broker's; the sealed runner (lb/bench_runner) gets one of
# its own instead: docker-compose.bench-net.yml, selected AFTER the broker override, on an EXTERNAL network
# you create once (docker network create --subnet <a free private /28> <name>). lb/bench_runner/.env then
# names the same network as its BENCH_UPSTREAM_NETWORK. Without the broker, leave both lines out: the
# runner's egress joins the LB's own network.
# COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:docker-compose.broker.yml:docker-compose.bench-net.yml
# LB_BENCH_NETWORK=

# ── a node the LB requests on ITS OWN box: ONLY with the broker override (or another that names the LB's networks) ──
# A one-host node of the fleet (control local) on this LB's host publishes on loopback only, which the LB's container
# cannot dial: docker-compose.node.yml puts the LB on the node's network instead, selected AFTER the broker override.
# LB_NODE_NETWORK is the node's EDGE_NODE_NETWORK (its server file, deploy/standalone/.env) — create it once
# (docker network create <name>) before the LB starts with it; the fleet file's url for that cluster is then
# http://<the node's EDGE_NODE_NAME>:8000. Several overrides chain in that order, e.g.
# COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:docker-compose.broker.yml:docker-compose.bench-net.yml:docker-compose.node.yml
# COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:docker-compose.broker.yml:docker-compose.node.yml
# LB_NODE_NETWORK=

# ── client containers of ANOTHER project on this host (e.g. a browser MCP calling the gui server) ──────────────
# The LB binds 127.0.0.1 only; docker-compose.clients.yml puts it on one more EXTERNAL network that such a client
# joins from its own compose file (reaching http://vllm-lb:8010). Selected AFTER the broker override, like the
# others; create the network once (docker network create --subnet <a free private /28> <name>).
# COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:docker-compose.broker.yml:docker-compose.node.yml:docker-compose.clients.yml
# LB_CLIENT_NETWORK=

# ── YOUR HARDWARE is NOT here: it is lb/fleet.yml ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# What your fleet IS — its boxes and their hostnames, every card and which serve, the cluster and the classic
# servers — is the fleet file: cp fleet.example.yml fleet.yml (or fleet.one-host.example.yml) and describe it.
# docker-compose.yml mounts it read-only (LB_FLEET). It replaces LB_VLLM_BOXES, LB_HW_HOSTS, LB_CLUSTER_HOSTS and
# LB_CLUSTER_URL: any of them left in this file REFUSES the start, naming where its value lives now.
# A GPU's PEAKS are its card's, per box: the hardware type each card of lb/fleet.yml names
# (vllm_lb/hardware/types/<type>.yml, every figure with its source). LB_GPU_PEAK_GBPS, LB_GPU_PEAK_TFLOPS
# and LB_GPU_PEAK_TFLOPS_PRECISION are retired: any of them left in this file refuses the start.

# ── the fallback: RETIRED (operator, 2026-10-17: "If can't run then can't run, no fallback box") ─────────────
# A request no server can take waits for one on its way, or is refused naming why and what is live. Every
# LB_FALLBACK_* key (URLS, MODEL, MAX_MODEL_LEN, BOXES, BOX_LABEL, IDLE_RELEASE_S, LEASE_MAX_HOLD_S) REFUSES the boot,
# naming itself (config.RETIRED_KEYS) — delete such a line from lb/.env.

# ── keep loaded: RETIRED (operator, 2026-10-17) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# No operator order pins a classic box's lease: a box's server is held by demand alone. LB_KEEP_STATE REFUSES the
# boot, naming itself (config.RETIRED_KEYS) — delete such a line from lb/.env.

# ── routing, leases, queueing and timeouts ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LB_IDLE_RELEASE_S=600                  # idle this long with nothing in flight → the lease is released
# (the window a lease requires is the lease model's catalog row, limits.max_model_len: LB_CONTEXT_LENGTH is RETIRED)
# LB_MAX_LEASES=2                        # how many vLLM leases may be held at once
# LB_LEASE_MODEL=                        # the model a lease declares; blank = the catalog's classic default
# LB_LEASE_MAX_HOLD_S=21600              # ceiling of one vLLM lease
# LB_LEASE_WORKFLOW=vllm_lb_lease        # the broker's workflow name for the lease job
# LB_LEASE_NODE_MODULE=system.vllm_lease # the broker's node module for the lease job
# LB_LEASE_BACKOFF_MAX_S=300             # longest wait between lease retries
# LB_LISTEN=0.0.0.0:8010                 # inside the container; compose publishes it on 127.0.0.1 only. Its port is
#                                        # the one the loopback names of LB_ALLOWED_HOSTS are allowed at
# LB_TICK_S=2                            # control loop period
# LB_PROBE_INTERVAL_S=5                  # how often each backend is probed
# LB_PROBE_TIMEOUT_S=3                   # a probe that takes longer counts as a miss
# LB_PENALTY_S=10                        # how long a backend that just failed is avoided
# LB_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_S=5                 # TCP connect to a backend
# LB_ADMISSION_WAIT_S=20                 # (broker on) how long an unheld turn waits for a claim
# LB_STARVE_AFTER_S=30                   # a turn waiting this long for a claim counts as starved
# LB_DRAIN_RETRY_AFTER_S=5               # Retry-After on a 503 `draining`
# LB_USE_UNLEASED_VLLM=0                 # (broker on) 1 = route to a vLLM nobody leased
# LB_VLLM_MAX_DEPTH=2                    # waiting+running past this demotes a vLLM below the fallback
#                                        # (the old name LB_VLLM_MAX_WAITING is still read)
# LB_BACKEND_TIMEOUT_S=3600              # the old name of LB_FIRST_BYTE_TIMEOUT_S (still read)
# LB_SCALE_UP_WAITING=1                  # queue depth that starts the scale-up clock
# LB_SCALE_UP_SUSTAIN_S=15               # how long that depth must hold before a second lease is asked for

# ── the cluster's knobs (vllm_lb/config.py); the panel's own settings win over these ──────────
# (what a box must have free is what the selection COSTS, per rank role — the catalog's memory footprint; a selection
#  the LB cannot size is refused naming why. LB_CLUSTER_MIN_MEM_GIB is RETIRED)
# (the concurrency and context a model is offered are its catalog row's launch.cluster.tuned.max_num_seqs /
#  max_model_len: LB_CLUSTER_MAX_NUM_SEQS and LB_CLUSTER_MAX_MODEL_LEN are RETIRED)
# (the prefill budget a launch falls to is the fleet file's clusters.<name>.max_num_batched_tokens:
#  CLUSTER_MAX_NUM_BATCHED_TOKENS is RETIRED)
# THE PAIR'S RoCE PORTS (sent to every rank; tp-rank `tensorfold_cmd`). A TensorFold row whose engine_args say
# roce 1 (its one-shot all-gather) is REFUSED without them: TensorFold would silently fall back to NCCL. No default —
# nothing can find a bonded link's HCAs: name the bond's slave devices and the GID index their RoCE v2 IPv4 GID sits at
# (`show_gids`, or /sys/class/infiniband/<dev>/ports/1/gids/<n> + gid_attrs/types/<n>). Same on every box.
# CLUSTER_IB_HCA=mlx5_0,mlx5_1           # NCCL_IB_HCA's device list
# CLUSTER_IB_GID_INDEX=3                 # NCCL_IB_GID_INDEX
# THE BROKER WORKER'S BOOT BACKSTOP on each pair box (a systemd --user unit): what tp-rank `heal` restarts after it
# remounts a box's NFS shares. This deployment's unit name, forwarded to every tp-rank call; no default — unset, heal
# remounts and reports that no backstop was named. Only a fleet whose boxes run a broker_parrot worker sets it.
# CLUSTER_WORKER_UNIT=my-worker.service
# LB_CLUSTER_OBSERVE_S=5                 # SSH status reads: this often while anything is in motion
# LB_CLUSTER_IDLE_OBSERVE_S=60           # … and this often at rest
# LB_CLUSTER_LOGS_EVERY_S=15             # how often the head rank's log is read while it starts
# LB_CLUSTER_FINISHING_S=900             # how long a restart lets the turns on the pair finish
# An ssh cluster (the pair) keeps ONE reserve instead of the three below: its fleet file's ram_usable_fraction
# (MemTotal x (1 - it): the start gate, the floor, the KV auto pool and tp-rank's memory guard). These apply elsewhere.
# LB_CLUSTER_FLOOR_GIB=12                # broker_parrot's danger floor: under this a box sheds its LLM servers
# LB_CLUSTER_MARGIN_GIB=8                # how close above that floor still earns a warning
# LB_CLUSTER_START_HEADROOM_GIB=8        # what a start must leave free beyond what the selection costs
# LB_CLUSTER_KV_AUTO_LOAD_GIB=6          # room an `auto` KV pool leaves for the weights to load
# LB_CLUSTER_KV_CACHE_MEMORY=28000000000 # the KV pool, bytes, for a selection with no measured figure
# LB_CLUSTER_KV_BYTES_PER_TOKEN=16392    # KV bytes per token per rank, for a selection with no measured figure
# LB_CLUSTER_RESERVATION_MAX_HOLD_S=604800  # (broker on) a reservation's own hold ceiling (7 days)

# ── web search ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Claude Code's WebSearch is a SERVER-side tool: the CLI asks the ENDPOINT to perform the search
# (tools:[{type:"web_search_20250305"}]), which no LLM backend can do. The LB performs it against the
# searxng sidecar instead. LB_SEARCH_URL= (empty) disables the interception and forwards as before.
# LB_SEARCH_URL=http://searxng:8080/search
# LB_SEARCH_MAX_RESULTS=10
# LB_SEARCH_SNIPPET_CHARS=180            # of each snippet folded into its title; 0 = titles only
# LB_SEARCH_TIMEOUT_S=20                 # one SearXNG query, end to end
# LB_SEARCH_LANGUAGE=auto                # the language SearXNG searches in

# ── the panel's Connect dialog: how an API client reaches this LB (vllm_lb/connect.py) ─────────
# The dialog reads the page's own address and what its door answers; these say what the LB cannot see.
# Neither is guessed: unset, the dialog offers no such route and says so.
# LB_CONNECT_TUNNEL=127.0.0.1:8010       # the LB's address ON ITS HOST (compose `ports:`): the dialog offers
#                                        # `ssh -N -L <local>:<this> <host>` — no credential past ssh. host:port.
# LB_CONNECT_BASIC=1                     # the edge in front of the panel's sign-in also takes HTTP Basic on
#                                        # GET /v1/models + POST /v1/* (deploy/nginx-edge-vllm.conf $vllm_machine_path)

# ── hardware telemetry, the perf log, the stats ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# LB_HW_PORT=48291                       # UDP port the boxes' senders broadcast to (LAN-visible; see compose); 0 = off
# LB_HW_MAX_AGE_S=30                     # a box's live numbers go blank after this silence
# LB_HW_HISTORY=60                       # samples kept per box per metric (60 × 5 s ≈ 5 minutes)
# LB_STAT_HISTORY=60                     # samples kept per backend per metric
# LB_PERF=1                              # 0 = do not write the real-traffic performance log
# LB_PERF_DIR=/data/perf                 # one JSON line per window; lives on the state volume
# LB_PERF_WINDOW_S=60                    # the window's length
# LB_PERF_MAX_MB=256                     # the log is trimmed past this size

# WHERE CHECKPOINTS LIVE — required, nothing is assumed (a missing one is an error that names it).
# MODELS_DIR: where checkpoints are downloaded to and kept (the deploy/*stage* scripts write here; e.g. an HDD).
# MODELS_SEARCH_DIRS: where each cluster box looks for a checkpoint, best first, `:`-separated; `~` is the BOX's
# home. The first entry is where `stage` copies a checkpoint to (the fast local disk). The LB sends tp-rank the
# catalog's model folder under each of these.
# MODELS_DIR=/mnt/models
# MODELS_SEARCH_DIRS=~/models:/mnt/models
